course: dictionary
lesson: 3069
title: Eczema: a skin problem, not a catching one
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Eczema is a common skin condition. It makes patches of skin dry, red, cracked and very itchy. The sentences around the word give you clues about what it means and how it feels.
explain: «Healthy skin is like a brick wall that keeps water in and irritants out; in eczema the wall is leaky, so the skin dries out, cracks and itches.» Irritants are things that make skin sore. The brick wall is a comparison that helps you picture what goes wrong.
explain: Eczema often starts in babies and children, and it may come and go. «Nobody did anything wrong to get eczema; it often runs in families.» If you have eczema, it is not your fault, and you cannot catch it from someone else.
fact: Eczema is a common skin condition that makes skin dry, red, cracked and very itchy.
fact: Eczema is not catching: you cannot get it by touching someone.
fact: Soap, heat, stress or some foods can make eczema worse. These are called triggers.
fact: People often treat eczema with thick creams called emollients, by avoiding triggers and by not scratching.
fact: An «eczema flare-up» means a time when the eczema suddenly gets worse.
exercise: «Healthy skin is like a brick wall that keeps water in and irritants out.» What are irritants? | *Things that make skin sore | Thick creams used every day | Bricks used to build walls | Patches of skin that are healing
exercise: Sam says eczema is catching, so he keeps away from his cousin who has it. What is the truth? | *Eczema is not catching, so you cannot get it by touching someone | Eczema is only caught from food | Eczema is only caught in hot weather | Eczema spreads through touch, so keeping away is right
exercise: «Thick moisturisers called emollients every day keep eczema calm.» What do emollients do? | Cause a flare-up | Stop you ever getting a cold | *Help keep the skin soft and calm | Make skin red and itchy
test: Which of these is a trigger that can make eczema worse? | Singing a song | *Soap, heat or stress | Reading a book | Drinking a glass of water
test: «Crusty, weepy patches need a GP.» What should you do about crusty, weepy eczema patches? | *Ask a GP for help | Wait for them to become hot | Scratch them until they stop | Cover them in soap
exam: A child asks, «Can I catch eczema from my friend?» What is the best answer? | Only if you eat the same food | *No, eczema is not catching | Yes, but only in winter | Yes, if you share a towel
